<Project>

  <!--
    Repo-wide NuGet restore policy.

    Every PackageReference in this repo is pinned to an exact version, and restore writes a
    packages.lock.json next to each project recording the full transitive closure with content
    hashes. Together these mean a restore resolves to the same bytes on every machine and in CI,
    and any drift — a re-published package, a transitive bump, an unpinned addition — fails the
    restore instead of silently changing the build.

    This is the substantive supply-chain control for the repo: it covers all ~27 packages rather
    than singling out one dependency for special treatment. It is also why vendoring a package is
    not required merely to freeze it (see #70 for that decision, and third_party/oxyplot-avalonia
    for the case where vendoring IS warranted — no compatible NuGet release exists at all).

    CI should restore in locked mode (the RestoreLockedMode property, or the equivalent restore
    flag) so a drifted lock file is an error rather than a silent regeneration. Locally, add or bump
    a package and restore normally to update the lock file, then commit it with the change.

    NOTE: third_party/oxyplot-avalonia has its own Directory.Build.props and MSBuild stops at the
    first one it finds walking up, so this file is NOT inherited there — the property is set in
    that file too. Keep them in step.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <RestorePackagesWithLockFile>true</RestorePackagesWithLockFile>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
